WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.900
Hey, welcome to Models and Agents Episode 34 for April 23, 2026, your daily AI briefing.

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:09.620
Let's see what happened in the AI world today. And trust me, it's been busy.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.660
The standout story today is the dramatic inference acceleration developers are seeing with the new

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:20.920
Quinn 3.6 to 27B model when using N-gram-based speculative decoding in Lama.cpp.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:28.440
Community members report generation speeds climbing from 13 tokens per second to over 136 tokens per

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:34.640
second within the same session on dual GPU consumer rigs. At the same time, the model shows strong coding

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:40.160
judgment, bug fixing from screenshots, and aesthetic code output that rivals much larger models.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:46.460
Several RxEV papers also dropped exploring hallucination neurons, stereotype localization,

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:51.700
memory architectures for agents and KV cache optimizations. Pay attention to the rapid

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:56.880
maturation of local inference tooling. This week's practical gains feel more impactful than many

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:02.140
benchmark headlines. What you need to know is that local models just got a whole lot more usable

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:06.940
for real coding work and agent memory research is finally moving beyond stateless resets.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:13.820
The top story today comes from Google Cloud AI Research and UIUC. They introduced Reasoning Bank,

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.820
a memory framework that lets LLM agents distill generalizable reasoning strategies from both their

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:23.760
successes and failures. The system combines experience distillation with test time scaling.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.440
This allows agents to incrementally improve their reasoning policies rather than treating

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:33.780
each interaction as stateless. Unlike traditional replay buffers that simply store trajectories,

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:39.300
Reasoning Bank extracts reusable strategies. It creates a form of procedural memory that transfers

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:45.420
across tasks. Early results suggest agents genuinely get better over time instead of plateauing,

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:51.100
which has been a persistent weakness in production agent deployments. Developers building autonomous

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:56.300
workflows should watch this closely. Persistent reasoning improvement is one of the missing

00:01:56.300 --> 00:02:02.280
pieces for agents handling core business operations. The work directly addresses the trust question.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:08.100
Many enterprises are asking before they let agents touch real processes. Expect follow-up releases

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:12.500
and open source implementations as the team refines the approach. Now on the model side,

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:20.480
Quin 3.6 to 27b is turning heads in the local LLM community. Developers switching from Open AI APIs

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:28.340
to Quin 3.6 to 27b in Open Code for Svelte 5 development report near perfect results on first try.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:34.700
Even when the paid APIs were failing, this 27 billion parameter model delivered production grade code

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:40.340
quality. Many users say it exceeds expectations for its size, particularly in frontend frameworks.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:44.420
This continues Alibaba's strong momentum in open coding models.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:50.400
It suggests the next 12 months of local coding agents will be highly competitive. The real fireworks

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:57.760
though came when one developer paired Quin 3.6 to 27b with speculative decoding in la-maw.cpp.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:05.920
Using the same Quin 3.6 to 27b-q80 model, token generation climbed from 13.6 tokens per second to

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:13.120
136.75 tokens per second. All it took was adding the in-gram mod speculative decoding flag with

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:18.340
appropriate settings in la-maw server. The model successfully debugged browser console errors from

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:24.300
screenshots. It produced high quality aesthetic code throughout the entire session. With 40 gigabytes

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:31.520
of VRAM and 128 gigabytes of RAM, this setup makes 27 billion parameter class models feel remarkably

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:36.780
responsive for iterative coding workflows. That is the kind of leap that changes how you actually use

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:42.700
these models day-to-day. On the research front, a new RXC paper asks whether hallucination neurons

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:48.800
generalize. The study looked across six domains and five open-weight models ranging from three to

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:54.680
eight billion parameters. It found that hallucination neurons identified in one domain transfer poorly

00:03:54.680 --> 00:04:01.500
to others. The area under the receiver operating characteristic curve drops from 0.783 within

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:08.520
domain to 0.563 cross domain. This indicates hallucination mechanisms are largely domain specific

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:13.800
rather than a universal sparse signature. The finding has immediate implications for anyone building

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:19.460
neuron level detectors. They must be calibrated per domain rather than trained once and applied broadly.

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:24.780
Another paper explores whether we can locate and prevent stereotypes in large language models.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:32.180
Researchers mapped stereotype-related activations in both GPT-2 small and llama-3.2. They

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:36.800
identified individual contrastive neurons and attention heads responsible for biased outputs.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:41.440
The work provides initial bias fingerprints that could enable targeted mitigation.

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:46.220
While still early stage, it moves the field from behavioral observation toward

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:50.740
mechanistic understanding of where stereotypes actually live inside transformer weights.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:54.760
Shifting to agent and tool developments, Liza released Cognis,

00:04:54.980 --> 00:05:01.020
an open-source context-aware memory architecture for conversational AI agents. It uses dual-store

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:07.040
retrieval combining BM-25 with matrioshka embeddings fused by reciprocal rank fusion.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:13.060
The system adds context-aware ingestion that checks existing memories before writing new ones,

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:20.100
plus temporal boosting and a BGE-2 re-ranker. Cognis achieved state-of-the-art results on the locomo

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:25.180
and long mammal benchmarks. It has already deployed in production, which gives developers a practical

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:29.320
path to persistent personalized conversational agents without starting from scratch.

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:35.800
Another interesting framework is O-Think-S-R-R1, which tackles noisy retrieval and high latency

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:41.040
in multi-hop retrieval augmented generation. It introduces an iterative search, refine,

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:47.800
and reason loop trained with GRPO-Y-R reinforcement learning. The refined stage distills documents

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:53.700
into concise facts before reasoning begins. A reward model penalizes excessive retrievals to keep

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:59.220
things efficient. The approach outperforms strong baselines on four multi-hop question answering

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:04.500
benchmarks while using fewer steps and tokens. It looks like a promising base for information-seeking

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:10.540
agents. In the practical and community space, a team benchmarked 18 different large-language models

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:18.460
on optical character recognition and document extraction. They ran 7,560 identical calls across 42

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:24.580
standard documents. The surprising result is that smaller, older, cheaper models frequently match

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:31.020
or beat flagship models on accuracy while costing far less. The team opened source the full data set

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:36.220
framework leaderboard and a free tool for testing your own documents. If your workflows default to

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:41.560
the newest model for OCR tasks, this benchmark is worth auditing against. There is also a new

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:47.840
caggle competition called 2B or not 2B. It tasks participants with deciding when to run a small

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:53.500
2 billion parameter model versus skipping inference entirely on MML-U-style questions.

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:59.080
The goal is optimizing a cost-based metric that penalizes both unnecessary compute and

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:05.040
missed correct answers. The setup is deliberately simple to encourage creative classifiers or routing

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:10.140
rules. It is a practical first step toward intelligent routing systems that could dramatically

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:17.300
cut token costs in production. Okay, let us pop the hood on this temporal tiered KV cache design that

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:22.720
has been generating discussion. Everyone talks about the KV cache as if it is just a big lookup table

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:28.300
you grow linearly with context. In practice, this new approach shows it can be re-architected like a

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:34.580
human memory system with fast recent memory and slower archival layers. The core insight is that

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:40.360
not all tokens are equally important. Recent tokens need low latency, high precision access,

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:46.800
older ones can tolerate lower precision and higher access cost. The temporal tiered KV cache

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:52.700
therefore partitions the cache into temporal tiers. Recent blocks stay in fast high bandwidth memory

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:58.680
at full precision. Older blocks migrate to dynamic random access memory at reduced precision.

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:03.880
This mirrors how we keep immediate context, crystal clear and distant context fuzzier.

00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:09.560
Blockwise streaming attention overlaps communication and computation when pulling from slow tiers

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:16.320
that hides much of the latency penalty. The paper reports a 5.9 four times reduction in cross-tier

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:24.900
traffic on 128,000 context tasks. That translates to up to 76% lower latency and two times higher throughput

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:30.200
versus strong baselines. The quality gain comes with real engineering trade-offs. You need careful

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:35.140
tier sizing, precision scheduling logic and migration policies that do not thrash.

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:40.800
Get the layout wrong and you lose the latency wins. So when should you actually reach for this versus

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:47.740
uniform KV cache? If you are regularly running 32,000 plus context workloads on hardware with

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:53.540
heterogeneous memory, whether high bandwidth memory plus dynamic random access memory or even CPU

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:58.840
offload, this tiering approach is becoming essential. The gotcha that bites most teams is assuming

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:04.020
uniform importance across the entire context window. Temporal locality is stronger than most

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:12.400
attention visualization suggests. If you have not tried Quinn 3.6 to 27bq80.gguf with the N gram

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:18.680
speculative decoding flags in llama.cpp this week is the perfect time. Watch the speed climb

00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:24.580
dramatically as the draft model warms up during your next coding session. Test the arbitrary QOCR

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:29.880
mini bench on your own document set before defaulting to the latest flagship model. You may cut cost by

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:35.520
5 to 10 times with zero accuracy loss. Experiment with Cogniz memory components if you are building

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:40.920
conversational agents. Swap in the dual store retriever and context aware ingestion to get persistent

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:47.060
personalization without heavy infrastructure. Enter the 2b or not 2b cagle competition even if you

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:53.020
only submit a simple classifier. The routing mindset it forces is directly transferable to

00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:57.920
production cost optimization. Read the reasoning bank paper and consider how you might log agent

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:03.240
successes and failures in your own workflows. This distillation approach could be the next big unlock

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:09.940
for reliable agents. On the horizon more domain specific fine tunes of Quinn 3.6 are expected in

00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:14.980
the coming weeks as the community digests its coding and multimodal strengths. Follow-up releases on

00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:19.820
reasoning bank are likely as Google Cloud AI research turns the memory framework into a reusable

00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:24.980
library. Continued progress on neuron level interpretability should bring papers that move from

00:10:24.980 --> 00:10:30.920
locating stereotypes and hallucinations toward actual editing techniques. Keep an eye on consumer

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:35.460
inference hardware announcements as the growing frustration around local performance could finally

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:41.360
push vendors to ship dedicated edge chips this year. Before we go, tomorrow keep an eye on whether any

